🏗 Real Code Example
Step 1: Constructor Function
function Person(name) {
  this.name = name;
}

Ab is function ke paas ek property hoti hai:
console.log(Person.prototype);


👉 Ye ek object hai
👉 Future objects isi se inherit karenge

Step 2: Method add karte hain prototype me
Person.prototype.sayHello = function() {
  console.log("Hello, my name is " + this.name);
};

Step 3: Object create karte hain
const user1 = new Person("Nishar");


Ab kya hua?

Internally JavaScript ne ye kiya:

user1.__proto__ = Person.prototype

Proof
console.log(user1.__proto__ === Person.prototype);
// true


🔥 Ye sabse important line hai.

🎯 Visual Diagram
Person (function)
   |
   |---- prototype  ----> { sayHello() }

user1 (object)
   |
   |---- __proto__  ----> Person.prototype

🧬 Key Difference Deep Understanding
✅ prototype

Sirf functions ke paas hota hai

Jab new use hota hai tab kaam karta hai

Future instances ko inherit karata hai

Example:

console.log(typeof Person.prototype);
// object

✅ __proto__

Har object ke paas hota hai

Internal link hota hai

Already created object ka parent batata hai

Example:

console.log(user1.__proto__);


⚠️ Modern JS me Object.getPrototypeOf() use karna better hai.

Object.getPrototypeOf(user1);

🏢 Real Life Use
1️⃣ Memory Optimization

Agar tum method constructor ke andar banaoge:

function Car() {
  this.start = function() {
    console.log("Start");
  };
}


Har object apna alag copy banayega ❌ (memory waste)

Better way:

Car.prototype.start = function() {
  console.log("Start");
};


Sab objects same function share karenge ✅

🧨 Important Interview Point
Q: prototype aur __proto__ me kya relation hai?

👉 Jab object new se create hota hai:

object.__proto__ === ConstructorFunction.prototype